summary:
- topic: Superintendent Transition
  text: The Board approved a separation agreement with Superintendent Matheney, effective
    August 8, 2025, and thanked him for his service and focus on DEIA. Dr. George
    Entwistle, a South Portland resident with extensive superintendent experience,
    was unanimously appointed as the Interim Superintendent for the 2025-2026 school
    year.
- topic: Board Leadership Change
  text: Following the approval of the superintendent transition, the board elected
    a new chair. Tandy Ratliff was unanimously elected as the new Board Chair, with
    members praising her temperament, experience, and dedication to the district during
    a time of transition.
- topic: Superintendent's Final Report
  text: In his final report, Superintendent Matheney highlighted district achievements,
    including SAT scores exceeding the national average and a 10% reduction in chronic
    absenteeism. He also warned of future challenges, such as potential federal funding
    cuts, the ongoing threat of gun violence, and the economic disruption of AI.
- topic: Artificial Intelligence (AI) Strategy
  text: Director of Technology Andy Wallace presented the AI Steering Committee's
    work, outlining a plan to educate students and staff on AI use. The strategy includes
    a 'walled garden' software approach with Magic School AI, developing student curriculum
    at all levels, and implementing a 'traffic light' system for teachers to clarify
    acceptable AI use on assignments.
- topic: Facilities and Security Upgrades
  text: The district is making significant progress on summer facilities projects
    funded by a recent referendum. Surveillance camera installation is nearly complete
    at Skillin Elementary and will proceed to other elementary schools. Roofing work
    has begun at Small Elementary, and work on secure vestibules at three elementary
    schools is set to start.
- topic: Staffing and Budget Update
  text: The administration reported strong hiring results, with less than 2% of teaching
    positions and only 10 full-time ed tech positions vacant, a significant improvement
    over previous years. However, the Finance Committee noted a preliminary budget
    deficit for the prior fiscal year and expressed concern over withheld federal
    funds impacting the upcoming budget season.
